Wait — I have a bug in my test. The `$normalized->close(); @unlink(...)` deletes `$clientPackPath` and `$modJarPath` and `$package->archivePath`. Then later in the cancellation section I try to reuse `$clientPackPath` — I recreate it with buildZip. That's OK. But there's a `return;` statement BEFORE the cancellation block — I added `return;` then dead code. That's messy: the cancellation part never runs because of the `return;`. I intended the cancellation to actually run. Let me remove that stray `return;`. Also, the anonymous class extends FakeDownloader whose constructor requires archivePath — passing $clientPackPath works.

Also, delete order issue: `@unlink($package->archivePath); @unlink($clientPackPath);` then recreate the client pack. But eager: the `getPackage` in the cancellation block will re-download the client pack (route fallback `$this->archivePath` = clientPackPath) — the downloader returns the clientPackPath as the "downloaded archive". Since the client pack was recreated, fine.

But careful: in the cancellation block, I build a manifest with `files => []` — so wantedMods=0, content = overrides only (the big world file). Then during buildServerArchive → writeArchiveEntry starts streaming bigWorld → first chunk hasn't been read yet... cancel flag set true BEFORE getPackage. So the FIRST isCancelled check in writeArchiveEntry loop triggers immediately → InstallationCancelledException. Good — tests mid-entry abort path.

Actually wait, there's a subtlety: with cancel=true from the start, getPackage's `$this->downloader->download($downloadUrl)` for the client pack — DownloadManager not used (FakeDownloader.download doesn't check cancel), returns path. Then collectArchiveContent checks isCancelled at loop top → throws BEFORE even reaching writeArchiveEntry. So the cancellation would be caught in collectArchiveContent, not mid-writeArchiveEntry. That still produces InstallationCancelledException, so the test passes but doesn't specifically test mid-entry write abort.

To truly test mid-entry abort, I want cancel to flip DURING the write. The FakeDownloader's isCancelled is called per chunk. I can make a downloader that returns false until the write starts... hard to coordinate. Simpler: make isCancelled turn true after N calls. collectArchiveContent calls isCancelled once per archive entry; for the overrides/ config/world there are 2 entries (config/meta.toml + world/big-region.mca) — collect checks per entry; mergeManifestFiles calls isCancelled per wanted (files=[] so none). Then writeArchiveEntry calls per chunk. Let me set a counter: return false for first 2 calls, true afterwards? But then writeArchiveEntry's first chunk checker call (call #3) returns true → throws mid-entry. Actually the first writeArchiveEntry call will be for the FIRST content entry in order. Content order: collectArchiveContent inserts overrides in zip index order: 'overrides/config/meta.toml' first (index order in zip: I created manifest.json, config/meta.toml? Let me check — in the cancellation block buildZip files order: manifest.json, then 'world/big-region.mca'. So overrides entries: world/big-region.mca. Only one override + no mods. So content = [world/big-region.mca]. collectArchiveContent calls isCancelled once (index 0 manifest? overrides path check). Then writeArchiveEntry: the loop calls isCancelled at top of while. So if I flip after 1 call, collect (call 1 → false, proceeds), then writeArchiveEntry while-loop first iteration isCancelled (call 2 → true) → aborts mid-entry (before reading). That tests the per-chunk abort.

But actually "mid-entry" more precisely means AFTER some bytes written. To flip after a couple chunks, I'd need the downloader to count writeArchiveEntry's chunks — indistinguishable from other calls. Eh, good enough: aborting at a chunk boundary of writeArchiveEntry is the mechanism; testing that writeArchiveEntry throws InstallationCancelledException when the flag flips during streaming. Let me make cancel flip after 1 call for the cancellation block.

Hmm, but there are also isCancelled calls in collectArchiveContent (1 per entry) and none in mergeManifestFiles (files empty means wanted empty → loop skipped). Order of operations in buildServerArchive: collectArchiveContent (1 call, index 0 manifest.json, still needs to continue... wait collect checks every index: manifest.json index 0 (call 1), world entry index 1 (call 2)). So to reach writeArchiveEntry streaming with flag flipping mid-entry: entries in zip: manifest.json (index 0), world/big-region.mca (index 1). collectArchiveContent: call for index 0 → false; call for index 1 → false. mergeManifestFiles: no wanted → no calls. Then content loop: isCancelled top (call) → false; writeArchiveEntry while-loop chunk 1 check: need it false to actually read data, then flip... This is getting complicated with exact call counting.

Simpler: make isCancelled return false the first 4 calls and true thereafter. Then:
- collect: calls 1,2 (false)
- content loop top: call 3 (false)
- writeArchiveEntry: chunk 1 check: call 4 (false) → reads+writes 256KB, progress report; chunk 2 check: call 5 (true) → throws mid-... after 256KB written. 

That's a genuine mid-entry abort. Let me restructure the canceling downloader with a counter.

Also remove the `return;` line before the cancellation block. And reorder deletes: I delete clientPackPath at end of first section, then recreate at the cancellation section.

Let me also reconsider: the first section ends with `@unlink($clientPackPath);` after creating package. Then the cancellation section recreates the client pack. Fine.

Also I should keep the test file ending clean. Let me rewrite the bottom half of the file properly.

Actually — there's another subtlety: `crc32($bigWorld) & 0xFFFFFFFF` — crc32() on 64-bit PHP returns an unsigned int (0 to 4294967295) already. `& 0xFFFFFFFF` is harmless. But `$stat['crc']` from ZipArchive is an int. With a big file CRC could be any 32-bit. Equality fine.

