Note: `FakeDownloader::download` returns `$this->archivePath` for any URL not in failedUrls and not in routes! That means an unknown URL "succeeds" (returns the pack path). So to simulate mass failure I must list the failing URLs in $failedUrls, or make them return a non-jar content... Hmm — for a mod to FAIL, downloadBestUrl must have every candidate throw. With the fake, a candidate that's in failedUrls throws RuntimeException → caught → next candidate; unknown URLs return archivePath (success). So to make N mods fail while 1 succeeds, I need their downloadUrl candidates in failedUrls.

For a mass-failure pack test: wanted=60 mods, each mod's resolved file has downloadUrl = unique URL, and each CDN fallback = unique URL. Put ALL the API urls + CDN urls into failedUrls except ONE mod. Then 1 mod downloads (returns archivePath content 'PARTIAL'... fake returns the pack path, so that mod jar in the normalized zip would be the client pack bytes — fine for the test, we just assert the exception message).

But that means enumerating 120 URLs. Manageable via a loop generating the data structures. Let me write the block:

```php
$massFailPackPath = $temporaryRoot . '/mass-fail-client-pack.zip';

$manifestFiles = [];
for ($i = 1; $i <= 60; $i++) {
    $manifestFiles[] = [
        'projectID' => 1000 + $i,
        'fileID' => 100000 + $i,
        'required' => true,
    ];
}

buildZip($massFailPackPath, [
    'manifest.json' => json_encode([
        'minecraft' => ['version' => '1.20.4'],
        'overrides' => 'overrides',
        'files' => $manifestFiles,
    ]),
    'overrides/config/x.toml' => 'x',
]);

$massFailData = [];
$failedUrls = [];
foreach ($manifestFiles as $entry) {
    $fileId = $entry['fileID'];
    $fileName = 'mod-' . $fileId . '.jar';
    if ($fileId === 100001) {
        $massFailData[] = [
            'id' => $fileId,
            'fileName' => $fileName,
            'downloadUrl' => 'https://cdn.example/mods/' . $fileName,
            'fileLength' => 1024,
            'fileStatus' => 4,
            'isAvailable' => true,
        ];
    } else {
        $massFailData[] = [
            'id' => $fileId,
            'fileName' => $fileName,
            'downloadUrl' => null,
            'fileLength' => 1024,
            'fileStatus' => 4,
            'isAvailable' => true,
        ];
        $failedUrls[] = 'https://edge.forgecdn.net/files/'
            . intdiv($fileId, 1000) . '/' . ($fileId % 1000)
            . '/' . $fileName;
    }
}

$massFailResolve = new ProviderHttpResponse(200, ['data' => $massFailData]);

$downloader = new FakeDownloader($massFailPackPath, [], $failedUrls);
$http = new FakeProviderHttpClient([
    $projectResponse,
    $filesResponse,
    $massFailResolve,
]);
$provider = new CurseForgeProvider(
    $http,
    $downloader,
    SECRET_KEY,
    $temporaryRoot,
);

try {
    $provider->getPackage('curseforge://314768');
    throw new RuntimeException(
        'A client pack losing most of its mods must be rejected, not partially installed.',
    );
} catch (UnsupportedModpackPackageException $exception) {
    if (!str_contains($exception->getMessage(), 'would leave the server broken')) {
        throw new RuntimeException('Unexpected partial-install rejection message.');
    }
    if (str_contains($exception->getMessage(), SECRET_KEY)) {
        throw new RuntimeException('API key leaked into error message.');
    }
}

if (is_file($massFailPackPath)) {
    throw new RuntimeException('Mass-failed client pack was not cleaned up.');
}

pass('client pack losing a majority of its mods fails loudly');
```

Wait: this makes exactly 1 mod (fileId 100001) downloadable (downloadUrl given, not in failedUrls). That mod downloads OK via the API URL candidate (returns archivePath). The remaining 59 have downloadUrl null → candidate = resolveDownloadUrl() (download-url GET via http fake!) → wait, resolveDownloadUrl calls $this->http->get(.../download-url) only when downloadUrl is null (in candidateDownloadUrls: `elseif ($projectId !== '')` → resolveDownloadUrl). That would shift a GET handler from the fake http client — the fake http has only 3 handlers ([project, files, resolve]) then subsequent shifts return null → `$handler instanceof ProviderHttpResponse` false → throw RuntimeException('Unexpected fake HTTP handler.')! That would abort the whole downloadBestUrl? No — resolveDownloadUrl catches Throwable and returns null (it's inside candidateDownloadUrls, called in mergeManifestFiles which runs BEFORE downloads). mergeManifestFiles → candidateDownloadUrls → resolveDownloadUrl → fake http get throws RuntimeException → caught by `catch (Throwable)` in resolveDownloadUrl → returns null. Good — no handler shift consumed because FakeProviderHttpClient throws unconditionally when handlers run out (it still records request and returns the shifted null → throw). Actually it throws RuntimeException → caught by resolveDownloadUrl → null. Fine.

But wait, there's a subtlety: each failed candidate download goes through downloadBestUrl → downloader->download(url) → will throw for failedUrls. But there are TWO candidates for each failing mod: the download-url GET returned null (no API URL), so candidates = [cdnDownloadUrl]. Only ONE failing URL each. 59 mods × 1 candidate each = 59 failed downloads → 59 `continue`s, downloadedMods = 1, wantedMods = 60. toleratedModFailures(60) = max(5, 3) = 5. failedMods = 59 > 5 → throw. 

But hold on: fake downloader returns `$this->archivePath` for url `'https://cdn.example/mods/mod-100001.jar'` — since it's not in failedUrls and not in routes. But also — the OTHER candidates: for fileId 100001, downloadUrl present → candidates = [api url, cdn url]. api url not in failedUrls → returns archivePath (success). Good, downloadedMods=1.

Also there's the 'overrides/config/x.toml' override entry → archiveEntry, not a download. Fine.

Also `$source` zip uses entries; `collectArchiveContent` reads manifest + overrides. content non-empty. mergeManifestFiles: wanted=60, resolved data → installed=60 (all have candidates). But wait: candidateDownloadUrls for the failing 59: `$file['downloadUrl'] = null` → resolveDownloadUrl via http GET. Fake http throws RuntimeException (out of handlers) → caught → null → candidates = [cdn url]. Non-empty. So installed=60, skipped=0. Then the download loop: 1 success, 59 fails.

`$failedMods = 60 - 1 = 59 > toleratedFailures(60)=max(5, floor(60*0.05)=3)=5` → THROW. 

Message contains 'would leave the server broken' → matches assertion. Good.

